  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R1, R3, R12, R18, and R19 establish mechanical alloying followed by SPS as a route from fine BCC HEA powders to dense, hard bulk materials, often with oxide precipitation during consolidation. R12 and R19 especially show oxide particles forming in SPS-consolidated BCC refractory HEAs, while R7 shows that protective alumina/chromia and spinel oxides in a closely related Al-Co-Fe-containing HEA can suppress oxidation. R6, R15, and R16 further support protection through continuous oxide or surface barrier layers, and R1 connects SPS-generated phase evolution with corrosion resistance. Thus an expert could reasonably propose high-temperature SPS of the MgAlTiVFeCo powder and attribute improved oxidation/corrosion behavior to densification and protective oxide phases. The exact 1000 °C optimum, identified AlV2O4/FeAl2O4/FeTiO3 phases, chloride passive-film response, biphasic parabolic kinetics, and quantitative density and hardness remain specific contributions of the later paper."
